{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QY5GiZGGh9Um9rr6pTCXLb","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t#!/usr/bin/env python3\n2\t\"\"\"Duplicate-code lint — copy-paste blocks violate one-fact-one-home (Thread 53).\n3\t\n4\tAn OPT-IN, product-layer harness step (stdlib-only, adapted from the proven\n5\tgilbert detector): tokenizes every Python source under --src and reports any\n6\twindow of --min-tokens consecutive *significant* tokens (comments, blank lines,\n7\tand indentation excluded) that appears at more than one location. Copy-paste is\n8\tthe failure mode this guards (the AGENTS working agreement: \"one fact, one home\n9\t— in code too\"); renamed-identifier near-duplicates are out of scope — exact\n10\ttoken equality keeps the check fast, deterministic, and free of false positives\n11\tworth arguing with.\n12\t\n13\tOpt in per repo via a stack-profile step (docs/stack.ini — survives re-sync):\n14\t\n15\t    [step:dupes]\n16\t    command = {py} scripts/check_dupes.py --src {src}\n17\t\n18\tThe tokenizer is the Python reference — swap it for your stack like the rest of\n19\tthe harness. Legitimate repetition is allowlisted, not fought: docs/dupes-allow\n20\trecords it one entry per line (# comments fine). Two entry forms are honored:\n21\t\n22\t  * FINGERPRINTED (preferred) — \"<fp>  a.py == b.py\": a 12-hex block fingerprint\n23\t    (a hash of the duplicated tokens, line-number-free) followed by the pair.\n24\t    It sanctions THAT block in THAT pair; NEW copy-paste between the same two\n25\t    files gets a different fingerprint and so is NOT exempt — it fails until a\n26\t    deliberate census line is added. Regenerate the fingerprints with\n27\t    --emit-census (WI-276).\n28\t  * BARE PAIR (legacy, coarse) — \"a.py == b.py\": the whole file pair is\n29\t    exempt, so any later duplication between the two files passes automatically.\n30\t    Kept for backward compatibility; prefer the fingerprinted form.\n31\t\n32\tBoth forms match the finding's POSIX pair as a substring, so an allowed pair\n33\tstays allowed as the files grow. Exit: 0 clean (or all findings allowlisted);\n34\t1 with one line per duplicated block naming both file:line locations, its length,\n35\tand the census line that would sanction it.\n36\t\n37\tContracts: IF-007, IF-027 — the interface seams this module declares (process.md §8; rows of record in docs/requirements/interfaces.csv).\n38\t\"\"\"\n39\t\n40\tfrom __future__ import annotations\n41\t\n42\timport argparse\n43\timport hashlib\n44\timport re\n45\timport sys\n46\timport tokenize\n47\tfrom pathlib import Path\n48\t\n49\t# Shortest duplicated run worth failing on, in significant tokens — the\n50\t# --min-tokens default. Tuned upstream (gilbert): a canonical lifted helper\n51\t# (a copy-pasted _load_csv-style function) measures ~38 significant tokens, so\n52\t# 30 catches it with margin, while idiomatic repetition — import blocks,\n53\t# dataclass field lists, argparse boilerplate — stays under the bar.\n54\tMIN_TOKENS = 30\n55\t\n56\tALLOWLIST = \"docs/dupes-allow\"\n57\t\n58\t# Length of the hex block fingerprint recorded in the census (WI-276). 12 hex =\n59\t# 48 bits: collision-free at the hundreds-of-blocks scale a real census reaches,\n60\t# short enough to stay readable on a census line. A fingerprint keys the FIRST\n61\t# window's normalized (kind, text) token signature plus the block's token extent\n62\t# — line-number-free (survives the files growing) but content- and length-\n63\t# sensitive, so new/extended copy-paste between an already-listed pair changes\n64\t# the fingerprint and is no longer exempt.\n65\t_FP_LEN = 12\n66\t_FP_RE = re.compile(r\"^[0-9a-f]{%d}$\" % _FP_LEN)\n67\t\n68\t\n69\tdef fingerprint(window, length):\n70\t    \"\"\"Stable 12-hex fingerprint of a duplicated block: its first-window token\n71\t    signature plus its token extent. Deterministic across runs and OSes (a hash\n72\t    of a repr of ints and str), and independent of line numbers, whitespace, and\n73\t    comments (those never enter the signature).\"\"\"\n74\t    payload = repr((tuple(window), length)).encode(\"utf-8\")\n75\t    return hashlib.sha256(payload).hexdigest()[:_FP_LEN]\n76\t\n77\t\n78\t# Token types that carry no duplicated *logic*: layout, comments, and the\n79\t# encoding/EOF bookkeeping tokens. NEWLINE (logical line end) is kept so a\n80\t# window can't stitch unrelated statements into a phantom duplicate — dropped\n81\t# NEWLINEs would let \"end of one function + start of the next\" look identical\n82\t# across files that merely share adjacent short functions.\n83\t_INSIGNIFICANT = {\n84\t    tokenize.COMMENT,\n85\t    tokenize.NL,\n86\t    tokenize.INDENT,\n87\t    tokenize.DEDENT,\n88\t    tokenize.ENCODING,\n89\t    tokenize.ENDMARKER,\n90\t}\n91\t\n92\t\n93\tdef significant_tokens(path):\n94\t    \"\"\"(kind, text, line) for each significant token in the file, or None when\n95\t    the file can't be tokenized (unterminated string/bracket, bad coding\n96\t    cookie, non-UTF-8). A lint surfaces bad input, never crashes on it — the\n97\t    kit's own convention (gen_arch_map catches SyntaxError).\n98\t\n99\t    `kind` is the token-type NAME (tokenize.tok_name[...]), not its integer id:\n100\t    the integers are not stable across Python releases (e.g. OP is 54 on 3.11\n101\t    and 55 on 3.12), so hashing them would make a fingerprint census generated\n102\t    on one interpreter fail on another (the gate job may run a newer Python than\n103\t    a developer). The names (\"OP\", \"NAME\", \"STRING\", …) are stable, so the\n104\t    fingerprint is portable across the version matrix (WI-276).\"\"\"\n105\t    try:\n106\t        with open(path, \"rb\") as handle:\n107\t            return [\n108\t                (tokenize.tok_name[tok.type], tok.string, tok.start[0])\n109\t                for tok in tokenize.tokenize(handle.readline)\n110\t                if tok.type not in _INSIGNIFICANT\n111\t            ]\n112\t    except (tokenize.TokenError, SyntaxError, UnicodeDecodeError) as exc:\n113\t        print(\n114\t            \"check_dupes: WARN - {}: could not tokenize ({}); skipped\".format(\n115\t                Path(path).as_posix(), exc\n116\t            ),\n117\t            file=sys.stderr,\n118\t        )\n119\t        return None\n120\t\n121\t\n122\tdef _windows(tokens, min_tokens):\n123\t    \"\"\"Hashable min_tokens-wide windows keyed to their starting line.\"\"\"\n124\t    signature = [(kind, text) for kind, text, _line in tokens]\n125\t    for i in range(len(signature) - min_tokens + 1):\n126\t        yield tuple(signature[i : i + min_tokens]), tokens[i][2]\n127\t\n128\t\n129\tdef find_duplicates(files, min_tokens):\n130\t    \"\"\"Duplicated blocks across the given files.\n131\t\n132\t    Returns a sorted list of ((file_a, line_a), (file_b, line_b), token_len, fp)\n133\t    with overlapping windows of the same duplicate merged, so one lifted\n134\t    helper reports once, not once per sliding-window offset. `fp` is the block's\n135\t    fingerprint() (line-number-free content+length hash), used to key census\n136\t    exemptions to a specific block, not the whole file pair (WI-276).\n137\t    \"\"\"\n138\t    seen = {}  # window -> (file, line) of first occurrence\n139\t    # (file_a, file_b) -> {line_a - line_b offset -> [(line_a, line_b, window)]}:\n140\t    # windows from one duplicated block share their line offset, so grouping\n141\t    # by offset merges the sliding-window hits into a single finding. The window\n142\t    # rides along so the merged block can be fingerprinted from its first window.\n143\t    pairs = {}\n144\t    for path in files:\n145\t        toks = significant_tokens(path)\n146\t        if toks is None:\n147\t            continue  # un-tokenizable file — warned, skipped (A1)\n148\t        for window, line in _windows(toks, min_tokens):\n149\t            if window in seen:\n150\t                first_file, first_line = seen[window]\n151\t                if (first_file, first_line) == (path, line):\n152\t                    continue  # a repeated window inside one physical block\n153\t                key = (first_file, path)\n154\t                pairs.setdefault(key, {}).setdefault(first_line - line, []).append(\n155\t                    (first_line, line, window)\n156\t                )\n157\t            else:\n158\t                seen[window] = (path, line)\n159\t    findings = []\n160\t    for (file_a, file_b), by_offset in pairs.items():\n161\t        for hits in by_offset.values():\n162\t            # Two *distinct* duplicated regions that share a line offset must\n163\t            # report separately, not merge into one inflated finding: split the\n164\t            # group into contiguous runs where the window start-lines are\n165\t            # adjacent (a gap > 1 line is a second block).\n166\t            for run in _contiguous_runs(sorted(hits, key=lambda h: (h[0], h[1]))):\n167\t                line_a, line_b, window = run[0]\n168\t                # Window count approximates extent: N overlapping windows span\n169\t                # roughly min_tokens + N - 1 tokens.\n170\t                length = min_tokens + len(run) - 1\n171\t                fp = fingerprint(window, length)\n172\t                findings.append(((file_a, line_a), (file_b, line_b), length, fp))\n173\t    return sorted(findings)\n174\t\n175\t\n176\tdef _contiguous_runs(hits):\n177\t    \"\"\"Split hits (sorted (line_a, line_b, window) tuples of one offset group)\n178\t    where the line_a values jump by more than 1 — the boundary between two\n179\t    separate duplicated blocks that happen to share a line offset.\"\"\"\n180\t    run = []\n181\t    for pair in hits:\n182\t        if run and pair[0] - run[-1][0] > 1:\n183\t            yield run\n184\t            run = []\n185\t        run.append(pair)\n186\t    if run:\n187\t        yield run\n188\t\n189\t\n190\tdef _canonical_pair(text):\n191\t    \"\"\"`a == b` with each side stripped, so a census pair matches a finding's\n192\t    pair regardless of incidental spacing around the `==`.\"\"\"\n193\t    left, _, right = text.partition(\"==\")\n194\t    return \"{} == {}\".format(left.strip(), right.strip())\n195\t\n196\t\n197\tdef read_allowlist(path):\n198\t    \"\"\"Parse the census into (fingerprint_or_None, pair) entries, one per\n199\t    non-comment line (blanks and # comments skipped), or [].\n200\t\n201\t    A line whose first whitespace-delimited token is a 12-hex fingerprint and\n202\t    whose remainder holds a \"==\" pair is a FINGERPRINTED entry — it sanctions\n203\t    ONE block of that fingerprint in that (canonicalized, exact) pair; listing\n204\t    the same fingerprint+pair N times sanctions N identical copies. Any other\n205\t    line is a legacy BARE-PAIR entry (fingerprint None) that coarsely sanctions\n206\t    the whole file pair as a substring, so it survives the files growing.\"\"\"\n207\t    if not path.exists():\n208\t        return []\n209\t    entries = []\n210\t    for ln in path.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\").splitlines():\n211\t        s = ln.strip()\n212\t        if not s or s.startswith(\"#\"):\n213\t            continue\n214\t        parts = s.split(None, 1)\n215\t        head = parts[0]\n216\t        rest = parts[1].strip() if len(parts) > 1 else \"\"\n217\t        if _FP_RE.match(head) and \"==\" in rest:\n218\t            entries.append((head, _canonical_pair(rest)))\n219\t        else:\n220\t            entries.append((None, s))\n221\t    return entries\n222\t\n223\t\n224\tdef unallowed(findings, entries):\n225\t    \"\"\"The findings NOT sanctioned by the census, in order.\n226\t\n227\t    Count-aware (WI-276): each fingerprinted entry sanctions ONE matching block,\n228\t    so N census lines for the same fingerprint+pair sanction exactly N identical\n229\t    copies — an (N+1)th copy between an already-listed pair is reported, closing\n230\t    the \"growth inside a trusted pair passes automatically\" hole. A legacy\n231\t    bare-pair entry matches any block in the pair (substring) and is never\n232\t    consumed. Each finding is (posix_pair, length, fp, file_a, line_a, file_b,\n233\t    line_b).\n234\t    \"\"\"\n235\t    budget = {}  # (fp, pair) -> remaining sanctioned copies\n236\t    bare = []\n237\t    for entry_fp, entry_pair in entries:\n238\t        if entry_fp is None:\n239\t            bare.append(entry_pair)\n240\t        else:\n241\t            budget[(entry_fp, entry_pair)] = budget.get((entry_fp, entry_pair), 0) + 1\n242\t    out = []\n243\t    for finding in findings:\n244\t        (file_a, line_a), (file_b, line_b), length, fp = finding\n245\t        file_a, file_b = Path(file_a).as_posix(), Path(file_b).as_posix()\n246\t        pair = \"{} == {}\".format(file_a, file_b)\n247\t        if any(sub in pair for sub in bare):\n248\t            continue  # legacy coarse pair exemption\n249\t        key = (fp, pair)\n250\t        if budget.get(key, 0) > 0:\n251\t            budget[key] -= 1\n252\t            continue  # a recorded copy of this block\n253\t        out.append((pair, length, fp, file_a, line_a, file_b, line_b))\n254\t    return out\n255\t\n256\t\n257\tdef _utf8_console():\n258\t    \"\"\"Emit UTF-8 to stdout/stderr whatever the OS console codepage is, so a\n259\t    non-ASCII path / title / registry cell can't raise UnicodeEncodeError on a\n260\t    legacy Windows cp1252 console (verbatim across the\n261\t    kit). Python 3.7+ streams expose `.reconfigure`; guard for the rest.\"\"\"\n262\t    for s in (sys.stdout, sys.stderr):\n263\t        try:\n264\t            s.reconfigure(encoding=\"utf-8\")\n265\t        except (AttributeError, ValueError):\n266\t            pass\n267\t\n268\t\n269\tdef main(argv=None) -> int:\n270\t    _utf8_console()\n271\t    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__.splitlines()[0])\n272\t    parser.add_argument(\"--src\", default=\"src\", help=\"source root to scan\")\n273\t    parser.add_argument(\n274\t        \"--min-tokens\",\n275\t        type=int,\n276\t        default=MIN_TOKENS,\n277\t        help=\"shortest duplicated run that fails, in significant tokens \"\n278\t        \"(default {})\".format(MIN_TOKENS),\n279\t    )\n280\t    parser.add_argument(\n281\t        \"--allowlist\",\n282\t        default=ALLOWLIST,\n283\t        help=\"census file; each line is a '<fp>  a.py == b.py' fingerprinted \"\n284\t        \"entry or a legacy bare 'a.py == b.py' pair, matched against the \"\n285\t        \"finding's line-number-free form (default {})\".format(ALLOWLIST),\n286\t    )\n287\t    parser.add_argument(\n288\t        \"--emit-census\",\n289\t        action=\"store_true\",\n290\t        help=\"print a fingerprinted census line ('<fp>  a.py == b.py') for \"\n291\t        \"every duplicated block and exit 0 — the census-regeneration workflow: \"\n292\t        \"review the lines and keep the sanctioned ones (WI-276)\",\n293\t    )\n294\t    args = parser.parse_args(argv)\n295\t    files = sorted(Path(args.src).rglob(\"*.py\"))\n296\t    findings = find_duplicates(files, args.min_tokens)\n297\t\n298\t    if args.emit_census:\n299\t        for (file_a, _la), (file_b, _lb), _length, fp in findings:\n300\t            file_a, file_b = Path(file_a).as_posix(), Path(file_b).as_posix()\n301\t            print(\"{}  {} == {}\".format(fp, file_a, file_b))\n302\t        return 0\n303\t\n304\t    entries = read_allowlist(Path(args.allowlist))\n305\t    failures = unallowed(findings, entries)\n306\t    for pair, length, fp, file_a, line_a, file_b, line_b in failures:\n307\t        # POSIX-normalized in the finding AND the census match, so one recorded\n308\t        # pair holds on Windows and Linux alike.\n309\t        print(\n310\t            \"check_dupes: duplicate block (~{} tokens): {}:{} == {}:{}\".format(\n311\t                length, file_a, line_a, file_b, line_b\n312\t            ),\n313\t            file=sys.stderr,\n314\t        )\n315\t        # The exact census line that would sanction this block — paste it into\n316\t        # docs/dupes-allow to make the exemption a deliberate baseline change.\n317\t        print(\n318\t            \"check_dupes:   sanction with census line:  {}  {}\".format(fp, pair),\n319\t            file=sys.stderr,\n320\t        )\n321\t    if failures:\n322\t        return 1\n323\t    print(\"check_dupes: OK - no duplicate blocks in {} file(s).\".format(len(files)))\n324\t    return 0\n325\t\n326\t\n327\tif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n328\t    sys.exit(main())\n329\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"62600159-c9ad-4406-ae07-01315a3d47f7","uuid":"c347bafc-7266-4009-b8a4-e76948c57cf6","timestamp":"2026-07-23T17:26:56.522Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Projects\\ai-template-trains\\p0-g3-WI-276-dec8\\project-trajectory\\scripts\\check_dupes.py","content":"#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Duplicate-code lint — copy-paste blocks violate one-fact-one-home (Thread 53).\n\nAn OPT-IN, product-layer harness step (stdlib-only, adapted from the proven\ngilbert detector): tokenizes every Python source under --src and reports any\nwindow of --min-tokens consecutive *significant* tokens (comments, blank lines,\nand indentation excluded) that appears at more than one location. Copy-paste is\nthe failure mode this guards (the AGENTS working agreement: \"one fact, one home\n— in code too\"); renamed-identifier near-duplicates are out of scope — exact\ntoken equality keeps the check fast, deterministic, and free of false positives\nworth arguing with.\n\nOpt in per repo via a stack-profile step (docs/stack.ini — survives re-sync):\n\n    [step:dupes]\n    command = {py} scripts/check_dupes.py --src {src}\n\nThe tokenizer is the Python reference — swap it for your stack like the rest of\nthe harness. Legitimate repetition is allowlisted, not fought: docs/dupes-allow\nrecords it one entry per line (# comments fine). Two entry forms are honored:\n\n  * FINGERPRINTED (preferred) — \"<fp>  a.py == b.py\": a 12-hex block fingerprint\n    (a hash of the duplicated tokens, line-number-free) followed by the pair.\n    It sanctions THAT block in THAT pair; NEW copy-paste between the same two\n    files gets a different fingerprint and so is NOT exempt — it fails until a\n    deliberate census line is added. Regenerate the fingerprints with\n    --emit-census (WI-276).\n  * BARE PAIR (legacy, coarse) — \"a.py == b.py\": the whole file pair is\n    exempt, so any later duplication between the two files passes automatically.\n    Kept for backward compatibility; prefer the fingerprinted form.\n\nBoth forms match the finding's POSIX pair as a substring, so an allowed pair\nstays allowed as the files grow. Exit: 0 clean (or all findings allowlisted);\n1 with one line per duplicated block naming both file:line locations, its length,\nand the census line that would sanction it.\n\nContracts: IF-007, IF-027 — the interface seams this module declares (process.md §8; rows of record in docs/requirements/interfaces.csv).\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport argparse\nimport hashlib\nimport re\nimport sys\nimport tokenize\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\n# Shortest duplicated run worth failing on, in significant tokens — the\n# --min-tokens default. Tuned upstream (gilbert): a canonical lifted helper\n# (a copy-pasted _load_csv-style function) measures ~38 significant tokens, so\n# 30 catches it with margin, while idiomatic repetition — import blocks,\n# dataclass field lists, argparse boilerplate — stays under the bar.\nMIN_TOKENS = 30\n\nALLOWLIST = \"docs/dupes-allow\"\n\n# Length of the hex block fingerprint recorded in the census (WI-276). 12 hex =\n# 48 bits: collision-free at the hundreds-of-blocks scale a real census reaches,\n# short enough to stay readable on a census line. A fingerprint keys the FIRST\n# window's normalized (kind, text) token signature plus the block's token extent\n# — line-number-free (survives the files growing) but content- and length-\n# sensitive, so new/extended copy-paste between an already-listed pair changes\n# the fingerprint and is no longer exempt.\n_FP_LEN = 12\n_FP_RE = re.compile(r\"^[0-9a-f]{%d}$\" % _FP_LEN)\n\n\ndef fingerprint(window, length):\n    \"\"\"Stable 12-hex fingerprint of a duplicated block: its first-window token\n    signature plus its token extent. Deterministic across runs and OSes (a hash\n    of a repr of ints and str), and independent of line numbers, whitespace, and\n    comments (those never enter the signature).\"\"\"\n    payload = repr((tuple(window), length)).encode(\"utf-8\")\n    return hashlib.sha256(payload).hexdigest()[:_FP_LEN]\n\n\n# Token types that carry no duplicated *logic*: layout, comments, and the\n# encoding/EOF bookkeeping tokens. NEWLINE (logical line end) is kept so a\n# window can't stitch unrelated statements into a phantom duplicate — dropped\n# NEWLINEs would let \"end of one function + start of the next\" look identical\n# across files that merely share adjacent short functions.\n_INSIGNIFICANT = {\n    tokenize.COMMENT,\n    tokenize.NL,\n    tokenize.INDENT,\n    tokenize.DEDENT,\n    tokenize.ENCODING,\n    tokenize.ENDMARKER,\n}\n\n\ndef significant_tokens(path):\n    \"\"\"(kind, text, line) for each significant token in the file, or None when\n    the file can't be tokenized (unterminated string/bracket, bad coding\n    cookie, non-UTF-8). A lint surfaces bad input, never crashes on it — the\n    kit's own convention (gen_arch_map catches SyntaxError).\n\n    `kind` is the token-type NAME (tokenize.tok_name[...]), not its integer id:\n    the integers are not stable across Python releases (e.g. OP is 54 on 3.11\n    and 55 on 3.12), so hashing them would make a fingerprint census generated\n    on one interpreter fail on another (the gate job may run a newer Python than\n    a developer). The names (\"OP\", \"NAME\", \"STRING\", …) are stable, so the\n    fingerprint is portable across the version matrix (WI-276).\"\"\"\n    try:\n        with open(path, \"rb\") as handle:\n            return [\n                (tokenize.tok_name[tok.type], tok.string, tok.start[0])\n                for tok in tokenize.tokenize(handle.readline)\n                if tok.type not in _INSIGNIFICANT\n            ]\n    except (tokenize.TokenError, SyntaxError, UnicodeDecodeError) as exc:\n        print(\n            \"check_dupes: WARN - {}: could not tokenize ({}); skipped\".format(\n                Path(path).as_posix(), exc\n            ),\n            file=sys.stderr,\n        )\n        return None\n\n\ndef _windows(tokens, min_tokens):\n    \"\"\"Hashable min_tokens-wide windows keyed to their starting line.\"\"\"\n    signature = [(kind
